{"name":"Atlantis","hex":"#336172","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Atlantis","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A mythical country said to have sunk into the Atlantic Ocean."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Atlantis"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atlantis","blocks":[{"text":"Atlantis is a fictional island mentioned in Plato's works Timaeus and Critias as part of an allegory on the hubris of nations. By describing Atlantis as a naval empire from the west that had conquered most of Europe and Libya, Plato purposely created a literary contrast with the Achaemenid Empire, the great land-based power that ruled the east. In Plato's account, Atlantis loses divine favor after an ill-fated campaign against a fictionalized Athens, and subsequently submerges into the Atlantic Ocean. By portraying the victorious Athens in the image of his ideal state from the Republic, Plato intended the Atlantis story to bear witness to the superiority of his concept of a state."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Atlantis_Kircher_Mundus_subterraneus_1678.jpg/330px-Atlantis_Kircher_Mundus_subterraneus_1678.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantis","wikidata":"Q25373"},{"title":"Atlantis (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Atlantis is a British fantasy-adventure television programme inspired by Greek mythology and created by Johnny Capps and Julian Murphy with Howard Overman. It premiered on 28 September 2013 on BBC One. In the show, submarine pilot Jason washes up on the shores of legendary Atlantis and must navigate the powerful leaders of the mythological realm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/50/Atlantis_2013_tv_series_titlecard.jpg/330px-Atlantis_2013_tv_series_titlecard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantis_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q14955420"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Platon.png/120px-Platon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Platon.png/250px-Platon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Platon.png/120px-Platon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlaton.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Plato_Timaeus.jpg/500px-Plato_Timaeus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Plato_Timaeus.jpg/500px-Plato_Timaeus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A fifteenth-century Latin translation of Plato's Timaeus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlato_Timaeus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg/500px-Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg/1280px-Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg/500px-Crop_of_ISS067-E-153820_Santorini_caldera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Santorini caldera on 24 June 2022, taken from the International Space Station. From the Minoan eruption event, and the 1964 discovery of Akrotiri on the island, this location is one of many sites purporte